.TH SPACEGL_VULKAN 1 "2026-04-19" "SpaceGL @VERSION@" "SpaceGL Manual"
.SH NAME
spacegl_vulkan \- SpaceGL Vulkan 3D rendering frontend
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spacegl_vulkan
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B spacegl_vulkan
is the high-performance Vulkan-based 3D rendering frontend for the
SpaceGL engine. It uses GLFW for window management and renders all
galaxy objects (ships, torpedoes, Quantum Cores, particle effects)
using a custom Vulkan pipeline with SPIR-V compiled shaders.
.PP
Features include HDR rendering, multi-pass Bloom with Gaussian blur,
ACES Filmic Tone Mapping, compute-shader-driven particle systems, and
wireframe cloaking effects for Romulan vessels.
.PP
This binary is typically launched automatically by
.BR spacegl_client (1)
when the
.B vk
argument is specified. It can also be started independently to attach
to an existing shared memory segment.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-screenshot " FILE"
Capture the backbuffer of frame N as PPM and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-screenshot\-frame " N"
Frame to capture (default 30).
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " N"
Fixed RNG seed for deterministic starfield generation (golden render test).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-rotate
Disable the automatic camera orbit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-msaa
Disable multisampling (forces 1 sample for deterministic golden render testing).
.TP
.BI \-\-window " WxH"
Window extent (default 1280x720).
.TP
.BI \-\-bench\-frames " N"
Time N frames and exit, printing one line: [BENCH] renderer, window size,
objects, frame time average, p50, p95, p99, max (ms) and fps. Each frame
waits for the GPU to finish it, so the time is its whole cost (CPU and GPU,
no overlap between frames); the ~144 fps frame limiter is off. A missing shared-memory segment is
created empty.
.TP
.BI \-\-bench\-warmup " W"
Frames before the timed ones (default 30).
.TP
.BI \-\-bench\-scene " N"
Fill the empty segment with a busy quadrant: the captain's ship and N\-1
objects of the common kinds at fixed places (up to 256), the same scene on
every run. Also with
.BR \-\-screenshot .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-h\fR,\fB \-\-help
Print usage summary and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SPACEGL_GPD
Unset or 1: the GPU-driven render path (the default), which falls back to
the CPU-driven path when the driver or the device cannot run it (Vulkan
older than 1.3, no graphics+compute queue family, no synchronization2 or
dynamicRendering, a pipeline that cannot be created). 0: the CPU-driven
path. With an explicit 1, a
.B \-\-screenshot
run exits with an error when the GPU-driven path cannot start.
.TP
.B SPACEGL_VK_DEVICE
The Vulkan device to use: its index, or a part of its name (any case).
Without it the viewer prefers a discrete GPU, then an integrated one, then
the rest, a software driver (llvmpipe/lavapipe) last, and with the
GPU-driven path a device that can run it; the choice is printed at start
("[VK] device N of M: NAME (type, Vulkan x.y.z)").
.TP
.B SPACEGL_VALIDATION
1 requests the Vulkan validation layer, 0 disables it (on by default in
Debug builds).
.TP
.B SPACEGL_NO_MSAA
1 disables multisampling (like
.BR \-\-no\-msaa ).
.TP
.B SPACEGL_VK_GPU_TIMES
1 measures the GPU time of each section of the frame with timestamp
queries and prints a "[GPU-TIMES]" line every 120 frames (with
.BR \-\-bench\-frames ,
one line for the timed frames; there the sections are measured even
without the variable). On the CPU-driven path, with the variable set, the
objects are split by kind (ships, stars, planets, bases, asteroids,
comets, nebulae, other) and the effects (torpedoes, beams, explosions,
shields) are a section of their own: one timestamp after every object,
which itself costs GPU time, so compare the shares, not the total with a
run without it.
.TP
.B SPACEGL_VK_GPU_TIMES_DRAWS
With
.B SPACEGL_VK_GPU_TIMES=1
on the CPU-driven path, a kind of object (ships, stars, planets, bases,
asteroids, comets, nebulae, other): every draw of that kind is timed on its
own, and the "[GPU-TIMES]" line shows the time by pipeline and size of
draw ("bases:wire/12288": wireframe pipeline, 12 288 indices).
.TP
.B SPACEGL_SPHERE_LOD
0 makes the CPU-driven path draw every sphere with the full 64x128 mesh.
By default it draws a coarser level (32x64, 16x32 or 8x16) when the
sphere is small enough on screen for the difference to stay within half a
pixel (for a wireframe sphere, while its lines stay at most one pixel
apart); with
.BR \-\-bench\-frames ,
a "[SPHERE-LOD]" line gives the spheres a frame by level and a
"[CPU-CMDS]" line the draws, the pipeline binds and changes, by kind of
object.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/spacegl_vulkan
Vulkan renderer executable.
.TP
.I /usr/share/spacegl/shaders/
Compiled SPIR-V shader files.
